1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,883
Picture your shop on its busiest day.

2
00:00:02,883 --> 00:00:05,459
One server answers every single visitor.

3
00:00:05,459 --> 00:00:07,198
Then that one server stops.

4
00:00:07,198 --> 00:00:08,679
The whole site is gone,

5
00:00:08,679 --> 00:00:09,774
with one machine,

6
00:00:09,774 --> 00:00:10,804
and one mistake.

7
00:00:11,354 --> 00:00:14,331
The fix is old and simple: keep several copies,

8
00:00:14,331 --> 00:00:17,434
and let engine X share the visitors between them.

9
00:00:17,984 --> 00:00:20,455
Today you build a pool of three backends,

10
00:00:20,455 --> 00:00:21,962
watch the traffic rotate,

11
00:00:21,962 --> 00:00:23,529
switch one off on purpose,

12
00:00:23,529 --> 00:00:25,579
and see that nobody gets an error.

13
00:00:26,129 --> 00:00:27,835
Share it, survive it, back it up.

14
00:00:29,385 --> 00:00:32,457
Here is what to expect, from the first minute to the last.

15
00:00:33,007 --> 00:00:37,871
One: what load balancing is, and how engine X notices that a backend is sick.

16
00:00:38,421 --> 00:00:43,072
Two: a live demo. We start with a single backend, and ask it six times.

17
00:00:43,622 --> 00:00:48,720
Three: we build the pool, and watch round robin, then we stop one backend and keep asking.

18
00:00:49,270 --> 00:00:51,220
Four: we tune the failure rules,

19
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:52,012
add a backup,

20
00:00:52,012 --> 00:00:54,327
and prove it by stopping two backends.

21
00:00:54,327 --> 00:00:55,241
Then your turn,

22
00:00:55,241 --> 00:00:56,886
with a lab that judges you.

23
00:00:57,436 --> 00:01:00,287
This is lesson four of the web servers domain.

24
00:01:00,287 --> 00:01:01,341
You served files,

25
00:01:01,341 --> 00:01:02,085
you proxied,

26
00:01:02,085 --> 00:01:03,014
you secured it.

27
00:01:03,014 --> 00:01:04,626
Today you make it survive.

28
00:01:05,176 --> 00:01:06,882
Share it, survive it, back it up.

29
00:01:08,932 --> 00:01:13,497
One address in front, several servers behind. Let us see who decides what.

30
00:01:14,047 --> 00:01:18,591
The pool is called an upstream. It is just a named list of backend servers.

31
00:01:19,141 --> 00:01:23,808
By default engine X uses round robin: first request to the first server,

32
00:01:23,808 --> 00:01:25,170
second to the second,

33
00:01:25,170 --> 00:01:26,401
third to the third,

34
00:01:26,401 --> 00:01:27,568
then around again.

35
00:01:28,118 --> 00:01:30,365
How does it know a server is sick?

36
00:01:30,365 --> 00:01:34,727
Honest answer: open source engine X only learns from real traffic.

37
00:01:34,727 --> 00:01:36,842
If a request to a backend fails,

38
00:01:36,842 --> 00:01:38,428
that failure is counted.

39
00:01:38,428 --> 00:01:40,939
This is called a passive health check.

40
00:01:41,489 --> 00:01:43,065
Two settings control it.

41
00:01:43,065 --> 00:01:45,888
Max fails: how many failures count as sick.

42
00:01:45,888 --> 00:01:48,580
Fail timeout: how long to leave it alone,

43
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:50,090
before trying it again.

44
00:01:50,090 --> 00:01:54,817
And a request that hit a dead server is quietly retried on the next one,

45
00:01:54,817 --> 00:01:56,721
so the visitor never notices.

46
00:01:57,271 --> 00:01:58,717
One more tool: backup.

47
00:01:58,717 --> 00:02:00,490
A backup server sits quiet,

48
00:02:00,490 --> 00:02:03,578
and only works when every other server is down.

49
00:02:03,578 --> 00:02:04,958
Active health checks,

50
00:02:04,958 --> 00:02:07,126
where engine X probes on a timer,

51
00:02:07,126 --> 00:02:08,834
exist in the paid edition,

52
00:02:08,834 --> 00:02:10,016
or in other tools.

53
00:02:10,016 --> 00:02:12,381
We stay with what you can run today,

54
00:02:12,381 --> 00:02:12,973
for free.

55
00:02:14,523 --> 00:02:19,600
Go to the lab folder.

56
00:02:21,150 --> 00:02:23,049
Look around. Type: L S.

57
00:02:23,599 --> 00:02:29,231
A config, logs, and three tiny backends already running. Each one only says its own name.

58
00:02:29,781 --> 00:02:33,429
Read the config.

59
00:02:33,979 --> 00:02:35,117
One server block,

60
00:02:35,117 --> 00:02:37,191
listening on eighty-two eleven.

61
00:02:37,191 --> 00:02:41,474
Look at the proxy pass line: it sends everything to one address,

62
00:02:41,474 --> 00:02:42,679
eighty-two twelve.

63
00:02:42,679 --> 00:02:43,482
One backend.

64
00:02:43,482 --> 00:02:45,222
A single point of failure.

65
00:02:45,772 --> 00:02:57,740
Ask six times.

66
00:02:58,290 --> 00:03:01,895
Backend A, six times. Everything lands on one machine.

67
00:03:02,445 --> 00:03:17,037
Ask each backend directly.

68
00:03:17,587 --> 00:03:24,094
A, B and C, all healthy, all waiting. Engine X just is not using B and C. Let us fix that.

69
00:03:24,644 --> 00:03:28,398
Open the config in vim.

70
00:03:28,948 --> 00:03:30,264
Go to line thirteen,

71
00:03:30,264 --> 00:03:31,317
the server line,

72
00:03:31,317 --> 00:03:32,961
and open a line above it.

73
00:03:32,961 --> 00:03:35,264
Write an upstream block named cart,

74
00:03:35,264 --> 00:03:36,843
with three server lines,

75
00:03:36,843 --> 00:03:38,224
one for each backend.

76
00:03:38,224 --> 00:03:40,592
Then go down to the proxy pass line,

77
00:03:40,592 --> 00:03:42,895
and change it to point at the name,

78
00:03:42,895 --> 00:03:43,224
cart.

79
00:03:43,224 --> 00:03:43,684
A name,

80
00:03:43,684 --> 00:03:44,671
not an address.

81
00:04:01,521 --> 00:04:09,158
Test, then reload.

82
00:04:09,708 --> 00:04:11,436
Safe, and applied instantly.

83
00:04:11,986 --> 00:04:14,781
Ask six times again.

84
00:04:17,531 --> 00:04:17,676
A,

85
00:04:17,676 --> 00:04:17,822
B,

86
00:04:17,822 --> 00:04:17,967
C,

87
00:04:17,967 --> 00:04:18,112
A,

88
00:04:18,112 --> 00:04:18,258
B,

89
00:04:18,258 --> 00:04:18,403
C.

90
00:04:18,403 --> 00:04:19,857
That is round robin.

91
00:04:19,857 --> 00:04:21,964
Same address for the visitor,

92
00:04:21,964 --> 00:04:24,144
three machines doing the work.

93
00:04:24,694 --> 00:04:28,406
Stop backend B.

94
00:04:28,956 --> 00:04:33,970
Backend B is down. A real server crash looks exactly like this to engine X.

95
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:37,080
Ask six times.

96
00:04:40,030 --> 00:04:40,880
Look closely.

97
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:41,665
Six answers,

98
00:04:41,665 --> 00:04:42,319
no errors.

99
00:04:42,319 --> 00:04:44,085
Only A and C are answering.

100
00:04:44,085 --> 00:04:48,663
The first request that reached the dead server was retried on another,

101
00:04:48,663 --> 00:04:51,409
and engine X stopped sending traffic to B.

102
00:04:51,409 --> 00:04:52,979
The visitor saw nothing.

103
00:04:53,529 --> 00:04:55,982
Start backend B again.

104
00:04:56,532 --> 00:04:58,024
After the fail timeout,

105
00:04:58,024 --> 00:04:59,710
engine X will try B again,

106
00:04:59,710 --> 00:05:01,007
and welcome it back.

107
00:05:01,007 --> 00:05:03,018
Nobody had to touch the config.

108
00:05:03,568 --> 00:05:09,221
Open the config again.

109
00:05:09,771 --> 00:05:11,190
Go to line fourteen,

110
00:05:11,190 --> 00:05:12,821
the three server lines.

111
00:05:12,821 --> 00:05:13,743
Replace them.

112
00:05:13,743 --> 00:05:14,524
On A and B,

113
00:05:14,524 --> 00:05:16,297
add max fails equals two,

114
00:05:16,297 --> 00:05:18,922
and fail timeout equals five seconds.

115
00:05:18,922 --> 00:05:19,276
On C,

116
00:05:19,276 --> 00:05:20,766
add one word: backup.

117
00:05:20,766 --> 00:05:24,171
C will now only work when A and B are both down.

118
00:05:34,921 --> 00:05:41,022
Test, then reload.

119
00:05:41,572 --> 00:05:42,490
Applied.

120
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:45,600
Ask six times.

121
00:05:48,550 --> 00:05:53,755
A, B, A, B, A, B. C stays quiet, like a spare tyre. Now the real test.

122
00:05:54,305 --> 00:05:56,524
Stop backend A.

123
00:05:57,074 --> 00:05:59,868
Stop backend B.

124
00:06:00,418 --> 00:06:05,005
Ask six times.

125
00:06:05,955 --> 00:06:06,084
C,

126
00:06:06,084 --> 00:06:06,729
six times.

127
00:06:06,729 --> 00:06:09,694
The backup woke up exactly when it was needed,

128
00:06:09,694 --> 00:06:11,758
and the site is still answering.

129
00:06:12,308 --> 00:06:14,932
Restart A.

130
00:06:15,482 --> 00:06:16,868
Restart B.

131
00:06:18,018 --> 00:06:25,037
Run the judge.

132
00:06:25,587 --> 00:06:26,422
Five checks.

133
00:06:26,422 --> 00:06:27,534
A pool of three.

134
00:06:27,534 --> 00:06:29,343
Traffic shared by A and B.

135
00:06:29,343 --> 00:06:30,873
The failure rules set.

136
00:06:30,873 --> 00:06:32,959
The site survives with B down.

137
00:06:32,959 --> 00:06:36,437
And the backup answers when A and B are both down.

138
00:06:36,437 --> 00:06:37,619
Five out of five.

139
00:06:38,169 --> 00:06:40,132
Clean up.

140
00:06:40,682 --> 00:06:44,884
Engine X stopped, all three backends stopped, and everything removed.

141
00:06:45,434 --> 00:06:55,824
Are the ports free? Count the lines.

142
00:06:56,374 --> 00:06:59,382
Zero. Nothing listens, nothing is left behind.

143
00:07:00,932 --> 00:07:04,303
Your task. Build a pool, survive a failure, keep a backup.

144
00:07:04,853 --> 00:07:08,885
One: lab up, and ask six times. Always A. That is the problem.

145
00:07:09,435 --> 00:07:12,313
Two: add an upstream with the three servers,

146
00:07:12,313 --> 00:07:14,080
and point proxy pass at it.

147
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:14,407
Test,

148
00:07:14,407 --> 00:07:14,865
reload,

149
00:07:14,865 --> 00:07:16,304
and watch round robin.

150
00:07:16,854 --> 00:07:21,227
Three: lab stop B, and ask again. No errors. Then lab start B.

151
00:07:21,777 --> 00:07:27,537
Four: add max fails and fail timeout to A and B, and mark C as backup. Test, and reload.

152
00:07:28,087 --> 00:07:31,373
Five: prove the backup, run lab check, then lab down.

153
00:07:33,423 --> 00:07:37,199
Here is where to find everything. Pause on this screen, and write it down.

154
00:07:37,749 --> 00:07:39,397
The lab lives on GitHub,

155
00:07:39,397 --> 00:07:40,564
at it dash waves,

156
00:07:40,564 --> 00:07:42,144
slash dev ops dash A I,

157
00:07:42,144 --> 00:07:43,654
in the folder academy,

158
00:07:43,654 --> 00:07:44,341
slash lab.

159
00:07:44,891 --> 00:07:50,502
Two files: lab one twenty-one dot S H, and shortcuts one twenty-one dot S H.

160
00:07:51,052 --> 00:07:51,959
Install it,

161
00:07:51,959 --> 00:07:54,846
on any Linux machine: sudo install,

162
00:07:54,846 --> 00:07:56,743
dash m seven five five,

163
00:07:56,743 --> 00:07:58,971
lab one twenty-one dot S H,

164
00:07:58,971 --> 00:08:00,208
into slash usr,

165
00:08:00,208 --> 00:08:01,198
slash local,

166
00:08:01,198 --> 00:08:02,188
slash S bin.

167
00:08:02,738 --> 00:08:07,492
Load the short names with: source shortcuts one twenty-one dot S H.

168
00:08:07,492 --> 00:08:08,698
You get two: lab,

169
00:08:08,698 --> 00:08:09,266
and N G.

170
00:08:09,816 --> 00:08:11,020
You need engine X,

171
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:11,890
python three,

172
00:08:11,890 --> 00:08:13,162
and curl installed,

173
00:08:13,162 --> 00:08:16,909
and ports eighty-two eleven to eighty-two fourteen free.

174
00:08:16,909 --> 00:08:21,058
The lab runs its own private engine X and three tiny backends.

175
00:08:21,608 --> 00:08:23,319
Lab up prepares everything.

176
00:08:23,319 --> 00:08:25,030
Lab check judges your work.

177
00:08:25,030 --> 00:08:26,550
Lab down removes it all,

178
00:08:26,550 --> 00:08:28,008
and stops the backends.

179
00:08:28,008 --> 00:08:30,035
Only run it on your own machine.

180
00:08:32,085 --> 00:08:34,154
Pass or fail. You need all five.

181
00:08:34,704 --> 00:08:37,435
Engine X runs with a pool of three servers.

182
00:08:37,985 --> 00:08:39,905
Traffic is shared by A and B only.

183
00:08:40,455 --> 00:08:43,292
Max fails, fail timeout and a backup are set.

184
00:08:43,842 --> 00:08:46,552
With B stopped, every request still succeeds.

185
00:08:47,102 --> 00:08:49,363
With A and B stopped, the backup answers.

186
00:08:49,913 --> 00:08:51,214
To pass, all five.

187
00:08:53,264 --> 00:08:55,035
Your turn, on your own machine.

188
00:08:55,585 --> 00:09:00,427
One: lab up, read the task list, and ask six times. Notice it is always A.

189
00:09:00,977 --> 00:09:05,244
Two: build the upstream in vim, test, reload, and watch A, B, C.

190
00:09:05,794 --> 00:09:09,079
Three: lab stop B, ask again, then lab start B.

191
00:09:09,629 --> 00:09:13,213
Four: add the failure rules and the backup. Test, and reload.

192
00:09:13,763 --> 00:09:14,979
Five: stop A and B,

193
00:09:14,979 --> 00:09:16,835
and prove the backup answers.

194
00:09:16,835 --> 00:09:20,035
Then run lab check until you see five out of five.

195
00:09:20,585 --> 00:09:23,743
Six: lab down, and prove all four ports are free.

196
00:09:24,293 --> 00:09:26,127
Pause the video, and begin now.

197
00:09:41,677 --> 00:09:42,552
Quick check.

198
00:09:43,102 --> 00:09:44,574
What does round robin do?

199
00:09:45,124 --> 00:09:48,302
How does open source engine X find out a backend is sick?

200
00:09:48,852 --> 00:09:51,306
What do max fails and fail timeout control?

201
00:09:51,856 --> 00:09:53,946
When does a backup server receive traffic?

202
00:09:54,496 --> 00:10:00,832
Objectives covered: M C thirteen, objective five. Health checks and basic load balancing.

203
00:10:01,382 --> 00:10:03,452
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

204
00:10:14,002 --> 00:10:17,372
